\section{\texorpdfstring{\pkg{cSEM}}{cSEM} R Illustration}
\label{sec:illustration}
\subsection{Getting started with R}
%This section illustrates how to apply PLSc using the \pkg{cSEM} package, starting from the very first steps.
%It assumes no prior experience with \textsf{R};
%readers already familiar with \textsf{R} and \textsf{RStudio} may skip directly to Section~\ref{sec:model-data}.

To use the \pkg{cSEM} package, users first need to install \textsf{R} and an integrated development environment (IDE) such as \textsf{RStudio}.
For the installation, we refer to the official guides for \textsf{R} (\url{https://cran.r-project.org}) and \textsf{RStudio} (\url{https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop}).

When \textsf{RStudio} is opened for the first time, it displays four panes (Figure~\ref{fig:rstudio}).
The two most important ones are the \emph{script editor} (upper left), where code is written and saved, and the \emph{Console} (lower left), where the code is executed and results are printed.
The \emph{Environment} pane (upper right) lists the objects currently loaded into memory, and the fourth pane (lower right) provides access to files, plots, and help pages.

Although code can be typed directly into the console, it is good practice to work in a script, since a script can be saved, shared, and re-run, which is essential for reproducible analyses.
To create a new script, click \textsf{File~$\rightarrow$ New File~$\rightarrow$ R Script}.
The typical workflow is to write code in the editor and run the selected line(s) with \texttt{Ctrl~+~Enter}.
Any line beginning with \texttt{\#} is treated as a comment: it documents what the code does and is ignored when the code runs.

<<label = intro-comment>>=
# This is a comment and is not executed.
1 + 1   # Code is executed; the result is printed in the Console.
@

Before \pkg{cSEM} can be used, it must be installed. The \pkg{cSEM} package is available on CRAN, so the most recent stable version can be installed with:

<<install-csem, eval = FALSE >>=
install.packages("cSEM")
@

Installation only needs to be done once per machine (rerun this line to update the package later).
Moreover, development versions can be installed from GitHub: \url{https://github.com/FloSchuberth/cSEM}.
After installation, the package must be loaded at the start of every new \textsf{R} session before its functions become available:

<<load-csem>>=
library(cSEM)
@

With \pkg{cSEM} installed and loaded, we can now import the data and specify the model, as described in the following sections.

\input{LaTeX/plots/rstudio_test.tex}

\subsection{Model and Data}
\label{sec:model-data}
The \pkg{cSEM} package \citep{csem} offers a variety of composite-based approaches to SEM including PLS-PM and PLSc.
%To illustrate the application of PLSc using cSEM, we use the data and model used in \citet{Cheah2026} to demonstrate SmartPLS.
%Similarly, we compare the results to those of the maximum-likelihood estimation as implemented in the R package lavaan \citep{Rosseel2012,Rosseel2025}.
Following \citet{Cheah2026}, our illustration of the application of PLSc using \pkg{cSEM} draws on the employee retention model introduced by \citet[Chapter~13]{Hair2019c}. 
%Following \citet{Cheah2026}, our illustration draws on the employee retention model introduced by \citet[Chapter~13]{Hair2019c}.
The model explains the effects of organizational commitment~(OC) and job satisfaction~(JS) on employees' staying intentions~(SI), with work environment perceptions~(EP) and attitudes toward coworkers~(AC) as exogenous antecedent constructs.
Five constructs are reflectively measured by a total of 21 indicators.
A detailed description of the indicators can be found in \citet{Cheah2026}.
The model is presented in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{"LaTeX/plots/retention_model_preview.png"}
    \caption{Employee Retention Model}
    \label{fig:retentionmodel}
\end{figure}

The dataset used for model estimation is publicly available from the SmartPLS website: \url{https://www.smartpls.com/documentation/sample-projects/employee-retention}. 
The synthetic dataset comprises $N = 400$ observations from HBAT Industries and was generated to satisfy the assumptions of factor-based SEM, including multivariate normality.
For this tutorial, we assume the data has been downloaded as a CSV file.
After downloading the dataset, it needs to be loaded into the R Environment.
The Employee Retention dataset is stored as an CSV File using ';' as the separator, thus it can be loaded into R using following command:

<<load-data>>=
# Import dataset
HBAT_SEM <- read.csv("data/HBAT_SEM.csv", sep=";")

# Select relevant variables
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM[, c("AC1", "AC2", "AC3", "AC4",
                   "EP1", "EP2", "EP3", "EP4",
                   "JS1", "JS2", "JS3", "JS4", "JS5",
                   "OC1", "OC2", "OC3", "OC4",
                   "SI1", "SI2", "SI3", "SI4")]

# show dimensions of the relevant dataset
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)

# count missing values per variable
colSums(is.na(HBAT_SEM_rel))
@

Since \pkg{cSEM} can only handle complete datasets, i.e., datasets without any missing values, we check the number of missing values per variable using the \fn{is.na()} function.
The output shows that the variables AC4 and EP4 each contain one missing values.
Before we can continue, we thus need to either apply imputation methods or delete the incomplete observations.
Deleting incomplete observations can be done via following command:

<<missing-data>>=
# filter the dataset, only select complete cases
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM_rel[complete.cases(HBAT_SEM_rel),]

#  the number of rows changed from 400 to 398 
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)
@

Since two observations contain missing values, all analyses in the remainder of this tutorial are based on the $N = 398$ complete observations.
When using their own data, users must ensure it is stored as a data frame or a matrix before passing it to \pkg{cSEM} functions; if necessary, the data can be converted with \fn{as.data.frame()} or \fn{as.matrix()}.


\subsection{Model specification}
In \pkg{cSEM}, models are specified using the \pkg{lavaan} model syntax.
The \pkg{lavaan} package does not need to be installed to use the syntax. 
In particular, the \code{=\textasciitilde} and the \code{<\textasciitilde} operators are used to define the relations between the constructs and their indicators.
Specifically, the \code{=\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify reflective measurement models, while the \code{<\textasciitilde} is used to specify composite models \citep[see][for an explanation of reflective and composite models]{}.
In addition, the \code{\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify the relations among constructs.
In case, an observed variable should be directly accounted for in the structural model, it must be specified as a single indicator construct, as common in PLS-PM \citep{}.
The \pkg{cSEM} specification of the retention model illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel} is as follows:

<<model-spec>>=
model <- "
  # Measurement model
  AC =~ AC1 + AC2 + AC3 + AC4
  EP =~ EP1 + EP2 + EP3 + EP4
  JS =~ JS1 + JS2 + JS3 + JS4 + JS5
  OC =~ OC1 + OC2 + OC3 + OC4
  SI =~ SI1 + SI2 + SI3 + SI4

  # Structural model
  JS ~ EP + AC
  OC ~ EP + AC + JS
  SI ~ JS + OC
"
@



\section{Estimating the model with \texorpdfstring{\pkg{cSEM}}{cSEM}}
\label{sec:estimation}

\subsection{Running the estimation}

The central function is \fn{csem()}.
The minimal input required for the \fn{csem()} function is a dataset and a model:

\begin{description}
  \item[\prm{.data}] A data frame or matrix with column names matching the indicator names used in the model description. 
  Possible column types of data proved are: "\code{logical}", "\code{numeric}" ("\code{double} or "\code{integer}"), "\code{factor}" ("\code{ordered} and/or "\code{unordered}"), "\code{character}" or a mix of several types.
  \item[\prm{.model}] A model string written in \pkg{lavaan} syntax, with \code{=\~} for measurement relations and \texttt{\~} for structural (regression) relations.
\end{description}

By default weights are estimated using the partial leasts sqaures path modeling algorithm ("PLS-PM"). 

To ultimately estimate with the csem function, we call it and save the results as \code{res\_csem}:

<<csem-est>>=
# PLSc-SEM estimation
res_csem <- csem(
  .data  = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model = model
)
@

To obtain a comprehensive overview of all results, including loadings, path coefficients, reliability measures, and $R^2$ values, use:

<<summary-rescsem>>=
# Full results summary
summary_res <- summarize(res_csem)
print(summary_res)

# For a more compact overview
summary_res$Estimates$Loading_estimates
summary_res$Estimates$Path_estimates
@

Note that the standard errors, $t$-values, and $p$-values in the \fn{summarize()} output are reported as \code{NA}.
The reason is that a plain \fn{csem()} call computes point estimates only.
To conduct statistical inference, the standard errors of the estimates must be obtained by a resampling procedure such as the bootstrap, which is introduced in Section~\ref{sec:measurement}.







